    <details class="faq-item" open>
      <summary>What is The Helix Center?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Helix Center for Interdisciplinary Investigation is a <strong>501(c)(3) nonprofit organization founded in 2012</strong> in New York City. It hosts free, public interdisciplinary roundtable discussions called "Theaetetus Tables" that bring together five distinguished scholars, scientists, and artists from different fields for unrehearsed intellectual exchange. Over <strong>{{ site.stats.seasons }} seasons</strong>, it has featured <strong>{{ site.stats.speakers }} speakers</strong> from institutions including Columbia, NYU, Princeton, Yale, Harvard, and MIT &mdash; with participants including <strong>Nobel laureates Daniel Kahneman and Eric Kandel</strong>.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Are Helix Center events free to attend?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">Yes. <strong>All Helix Center events are completely free and open to the public.</strong> You can attend in person at {{ site.address.venue }} ({{ site.address.street }}, {{ site.address.city }}, {{ site.address.state }} {{ site.address.zip }}) or watch via free live stream. All past roundtables are archived and freely viewable on the website and <a href="{{ site.social.youtube }}">YouTube channel</a>.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>What is a Theaetetus Table?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Theaetetus Table is The Helix Center's signature roundtable format, named for the Platonic dialogue investigating the nature of knowledge. Each Table seats <strong>five participants</strong> &mdash; leaders in their respective fields &mdash; for extempore (unscripted) interdisciplinary discussion. The five participants symbolize the five Platonic solids and the five qualities of mind the Center cultivates: <em>curiosity, playfulness, inspiration, reflection, and wonder</em>.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Who are some notable speakers who have participated?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Helix Center's <strong>{{ site.stats.speakers }} speakers</strong> include: Nobel laureates <strong>Daniel Kahneman</strong> (Economics, 2002) and <strong>Eric Kandel</strong> (Medicine, 2000); MacArthur Fellows <strong>Sara Seager</strong> (astrophysics), <strong>Vijay Iyer</strong> (music), <strong>Yiyun Li</strong> (literature); National Medal of Science recipients <strong>Sylvester James Gates</strong> and <strong>Barry Mazur</strong>; the most-cited neuroscientist in history <strong>Karl Friston</strong> (h-index 291); philosopher <strong>David Chalmers</strong> (who coined "the hard problem of consciousness"); and affective computing founder <strong>Rosalind Picard</strong>.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Who founded The Helix Center and why?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Helix Center was founded in <strong>2012</strong> by <strong>{{ site.founder }}</strong>, a psychoanalyst and clinical professor of psychiatry who observed how narrow specialization and the grant process were constraining creativity at professional scientific meetings. He previously co-founded the Philoctetes Center for the Multidisciplinary Study of the Imagination, then established The Helix Center to create "a university without walls."</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>How can I support The Helix Center?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Helix Center is a <strong>501(c)(3) tax-exempt nonprofit</strong> supported entirely by tax-deductible donations. You can <a href="/donate/">donate via our website</a>. Your contribution supports roundtable curation, professional video production, live-streaming infrastructure, and educational outreach.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Where is The Helix Center located?</summary>
      <p class="faq-answer">The Helix Center is located at <strong>{{ site.address.venue }}</strong>, {{ site.address.street }}, {{ site.address.city }}, {{ site.address.state }} {{ site.address.zip }}, on Manhattan's Upper East Side. Roundtables are typically held on Saturdays at 2:30 PM.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="faq-item">
      <summary>Can I watch past roundtables online?</summary>
